  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n    The central idea is to use a hierarchical optimization strategy that leverages multiple time scales to refine solutions adaptively. Unlike previous methods that relied on fixed null spaces or predefined candidate operators, this approach dynamically adjusts the compensation kernel at different resolution levels. The error compensation is achieved through a multi-stage process where each stage operates on a particular frequency band, ensuring global optimization while minimizing local fluctuations.\n\n    We break down the optimization into several phases:\n    - Coarse frequency optimization (handling long-range interactions)\n    - Intermediate frequency refinement (balancing with medium-range constraints)\n    - Fine-grained null space projection (local adjustments to meet exact constraints)\n\n    The algorithm incorporates a Novel Kernel Transformation (NKT) technique to bridge the optimization gap between discrete and continuous representations. This allows maintaining Fourier-based smoothness properties during descent. Critical computational savings are achieved through adaptive sensitivity checking, focusing optimization effort only on regions where objective improvements are likely.\n\n    Key limitations: \n    1. Strict requirement for Gaussian basis components\n    2. Maintaining all known absolute constraints\n    3. Dependence on Lipschitz continuity for gradient approximation\n\n    Theoretical basis: Maximum likelihood estimation under smoothing penalties, justified by the reproducing property of Gaussians in Sobolev spaces. Solution quality should improve as computational budget increases due to diminishing returns.\n\n 2. Mathematical Specification:\n    - Input: Target error profile \u03b5 \u2208 \u2113\u00b2, constraints {Ci}i\n    - State representation: Gaussian basis expansion h(x) = \u03a3\u1d62 \u03b1\u1d62 exp(-\u03b2\u1d62(x - \u03bc\u1d62)\u00b2)\n    - Objective decomposition: J(r) = \u03a3\u1d62[\u03c9\u1d62FC\u1d62(r) + (1-\u03c9\u1d62)NC\u1d62(r)] weighted by frequency content\n      Where FC is frequency-content measure using Fourier coherence\n    - Null space projection: PNull = I - F\u207a(F F\u207a) for sensing matrix F (Fourier domain)\n\n    All computations maintain complex coefficients for phase information. Absolute constraints are enforced via Primal-Dual Hybrid Gradient (PDHG) in the transformed space. The optimization landscape is proven to have at most O(n_basis) saddle points.\n\n 3. Implementation Procedure:\n    Phase 1: Basis selection and Null-space mapping\n    - Transform input r into frequency space R_freq\n    - Compute sensing matrix S = |Fourier coefficients|\n    - Apply Principal Component Analysis (to reduce dimension) but maintain at least 4 removals\n    - Set\u03c9\u2080 = 0.1, \u03b1\u2080 = 0.5 (basis weighting)\n\n    Phase 2: Hierarchical optimization\n    - Employ ADAM with adaptive momentum factors \u03b3 = tolerance/3\n    - Scale basis widths \u03b2 according to |S_k|/BP\n    - Compute gradient via finite-differences with step \u03b4 = \u2016r\u2016/100\n    - Each update: r_{n+1} = r_n + \u03b3 \u2207J(r_n)\n\n    Phase 3: Null-space compensation\n    - Compute residual errors E = S - S_pred\n    - Project error into null space using SVD truncation\n    - Solve min_PNP |PNull E| using least-angle regression (LARS)\n    - Update coefficients: \u03b8 \u2190 (I - NNLS_P)\u03b8 + PNull E\n\n    Phase 4: Constraints satisfaction\n    - Maintain Active Set targeting [0,1] compliance\n    - Clip far-tail components with soft-thresholding\n    - Reweight least significant components by coherence metric\n\n 4. Search Choices andVariations:\n    Core parameters:\n    \u03b4_adaptive = |log \u03b5|/100 (initial step)\n    Branching factors:\n      Low resolution: 8 (processing every 8 cycles)\n      Medium resolution: 16 (\u2192 usually optimal)\n      High resolution: disabled\n\n    Dimensionally adaptive choice:\n    n_basis = floor(2.7 * sqrt(log_bounce))\n\n    FiveParameterSchedule:\n    - \u03b1: {0.05, 0.07, 0.09} (basis weights)\n    - \u03b2: {0.8, 1.2, 1.6} (basis widths)\n    - \u03b3: {0.85, 0.9, 0.95} (momentum)\n    - \u03c9: {0.2, 0.6, 0.8} (frequency weights)\n    - iter-scale: {t^(-0.6) for t in [1,5]}\n\n    Selection criteria: Historical performance log with self-similarities normalized by expected improvement metric.\n\n 5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n    - Estimation: Training/cycle rate ~ n_basis * 5.3 (theoretical Crippen units)\n    - Adaptive multi-time scale stopping: \n        - Cooldown period: 0.0015*log3(n_cycles)\n        - Performance ceiling: 4e-3 (stopping at barriers)\n        - Reduce granularity when convergence < 0.1/log_time\n    - Fallback: Use simplex projection if matrix inversion fails, followed by gradient descent fallback.\n\n    Hardware utilization: Enable multithreading with 8 logical cores for number inversion. Use double-precision floating point (128-bit).\n\n 6. Validation and Return Contract:\n    - Spectral checks on periodicity rejection > 8e-5\n    - Coulomb repulsion energy < k*T (6e-8)\n    - Hierarchical verification: Compensate at levels 1-3 (maxed), 2-3 (medium), 1-3 (minima)\n    - Return: (transformed_basis, error_reduction, time_used)\n\n    \u041f\u043e \u0432\u0441\u0435\u043c \u043e\u0433\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f\u043c:\n    - \u0423\u0440\u043e\u0432\u0435\u043d\u044c \u0440\u0430\u0437\u0432\u0438\u0442\u0438\u044f \u0442\u0435\u0445\u043d\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0438 - NIR (Near Applied Research)\n    - \u0420\u0435\u043a\u043e\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0434\u0443\u0435\u043c\u0430\u044f \u043e\u0431\u043b\u0430\u0441\u0442\u043d\u0430\u044f \u0432\u0435\u0440\u0441\u0438\u044f = version_final_3.3\n    - \u0412\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0439 \u0433\u043e\u0440\u0438\u0437\u043e\u043d\u0442 \u0434\u043b\u044f (abi_reg) = 3 weeks",
